            <ArticleTitle>Opioids and the skin: &quot;itchy&quot; perspectives beyond analgesia and abuse.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1287-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Opioids are intimately linked to central pain inhibition and their abuse potential. Thus, peripheral opioid receptors in the skin have been studied initially with a focus on their peripheral analgesic properties. Recent results, however, clearly indicate that opioids play a specific role in skin homeostasis by modulating keratinocyte differentiation, wound healing, and inflammatory responses.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
